```

---

# 15. Why Does `umask 077` Produce 600?

File base permission:

```text
666
```

Mask:

```text
077
```

The group and others permissions are removed.

Result:

```text
600
```

Meaning:

```text
Owner  → rw-
Group  → ---
Others → ---
```

This is useful for sensitive files.

---

# 16. Real-World Example — Password/Secret Files

Imagine a Linux server stores a configuration file containing sensitive information:

```text
database.conf
```

If the file is created with:

```text
644
```

then:

```text
Owner  → read/write
Group  → read
Others → read
```

Other users on the machine may be able to read it.

For sensitive information, we may want:

```text
600
```

Meaning:

```text
Owner  → read/write
Group  → no access
Others → no access
```

A restrictive umask such as:

```bash
umask 077
```

helps make this the default for newly created regular files.

---

# 17. Real-World Example — Shared Company Server

Imagine a company has a Linux server.

The default umask is:

```text
022
```

An employee creates:

```bash
touch project.txt
```

The file becomes:

```text
644
```

Meaning:

```text
Owner  → read/write
Group  → read
Others → read
```

This may be appropriate when files are intended to be readable by everyone.

But suppose the company wants new files to be accessible only to their owner.

They could use:

```bash
umask 077
```

New files would normally become:

```text
600
```

This provides stronger default privacy.

